RISC-V: INT arguments and results are sign-extended by the side that produces them. Applied after perf-48; JIT, plus one runtime stub.
- The RV64 psABI has the caller sign-extend a 32-bit integer argument and the callee its 32-bit integer
  result, but the JIT did neither: genPutArgReg and genSimpleReturn skipped the move, and with it the
  sext.w, whenever the value was already in the ABI register. So every callee that took an INT parameter
  or call result to be sign-extended (perf-43) re-extended it in place: a sext.w in the prolog per such
  parameter, and one after the call per such result.
- genPutArgReg and genSimpleReturn now sign-extend an INT in place unless Lowering::IsInt32SignExtendedInReg
  already knows it is, for every target.
- Under NativeAOT, and only there, the other side relies on it: an INT parameter is taken to be extended
  as it arrives, and IsInt32SignExtendedInReg accepts the result of an INT call into managed code
  (IsInt32ResultSignExtended). NativeAOT compiles all managed code with this JIT; on CoreCLR, R2R code
  from another JIT and the runtime's own callers (CallDescrWorker, stubs) are not known to extend.
- Calls into native code keep the extension of their results: P/Invokes (GTF_CALL_UNMANAGED) and internal
  calls (FCalls and RuntimeImports, which carry GTF_CALL_M_NOGCCHECK), whose native declaration may be
  wider than the managed one (e.g. RhGetGenerationSize returns int64_t to a managed int). Helper calls and
  struct returns are not relied on, and a local mapped from a struct parameter's register keeps its
  prolog sext.w.
- Native-to-managed transitions in the riscv64 NativeAOT runtime were audited for INT arguments: native C/C++
  callers (reverse P/Invoke, InitializeModules, RhpCalculateStackTraceWorker, GC callouts) declare them as
  32-bit and extend them per the psABI; UniversalTransition, RhCommonStub and the dispatch stubs pass the
  managed caller's registers through; the catch/filter/finally funclets take no INT. The exception is
  RhpThrowHwEx, which passes RhThrowHwEx(uint, ...) the fault code RhpHardwareExceptionHandler stored
  zero-extended (e.g. 0xC0000005): it now sign-extends a0 first.

